@@ -23,4 +23,23 @@ resolvePointer(doc, ""); // doc itself
 - Only own members are followed (no prototype-chain access); the document is never mutated.
 - The URI fragment representation (RFC 6901 section 6) is not supported.
 
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies a JSON Patch (an array of `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`
+and `test` operations) to `document` and returns the result as a new value.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [{ op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" }]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+```
+
+- Throws an `Error` when the patch is invalid (not an array, unknown `op`, missing `path`, `from` or
+  `value`) or when an operation fails (unresolvable path, bad array index, failed `test`); a `SyntaxError`
+  when a pointer is malformed. A failing operation aborts the whole patch; nothing is returned.
+- Neither `document` nor `patch` is mutated, and the result shares no structure with either.
+- Removing the whole document (`remove` with path `""`) and moving a value into its own child are errors.
+- `test` compares as RFC 6902 section 4.6 says: object member order is ignored, types must match.
+- `__proto__` and similar keys are handled as ordinary own members and never touch the prototype chain.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test`.
